WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:05.020
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:10.080
Pose estimation is an interesting area of
research where we typically have a few images

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:15.670
or video footage of humans, and we try to
automatically extract the pose this person

00:00:15.670 --> 00:00:16.670
was taking.

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:22.070
In short, the input is mostly a 2D image,
and the output is typically a skeleton of

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:23.630
the person.

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:28.320
Applications of pose estimation include automatic
creation of assets for computer games and

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:33.930
digital media, analyzing and coaching the
techniques of athletes, or helping computers

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:39.640
understand what they see for the betterment
of robotics and machine learning techniques.

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:43.570
And this is just a taste, the list was by
no means exhaustive.

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:49.019
Beyond the obvious challenge of trying to
reconstruct 3D information from a simple 2D

00:00:49.019 --> 00:00:54.749
image, this problem is fraught with difficulties
as one has to be able to overcome the ambiguity

00:00:54.749 --> 00:00:58.659
of lighting, occlusions, and clothing covering
the body.

00:00:58.659 --> 00:01:01.629
A tough problem, no question about that.

00:01:01.629 --> 00:01:07.220
An ideal technique would do this automatically
without any user intervention, which sounds

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:09.000
like wishful thinking.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Or does it?

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.250
In this paper, a previously proposed convolutional
neural network is used to predict the position

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:22.280
of the individual joints, and curiously, it
turns out that we can create a faithful representation

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:26.530
of the 3D human body from that by means of
optimization.

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:31.460
We have had a previous episode on mathematical
optimization, you know the drill, the link

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:34.390
is available in the video description box.

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:40.120
What is remarkable here is that not only the
pose, but the body type is also inferred,

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:45.730
therefore the output of the process is not
just a skeleton, but full 3D geometry.

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:51.280
It is coarse geometry, so don't expect a ton
of details, but it's 3D geometry, more than

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:54.430
what most other competing techniques can offer.

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:59.630
To ease the computational burden of this problem,
in this optimization formulation, healthy

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:05.080
constraints are assumed that apply to the
human body, such as avoiding unnatural knee

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:08.269
and elbow bends, and self-intersections.

00:02:08.269 --> 00:02:13.070
If we use these constraints, the space in
which we have to look for possible solutions

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:14.070
shrinks considerably.

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:20.060
The results show that this algorithm outperforms
several other state of the art techniques

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:21.930
by a significant margin.

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:27.860
It is an auspicious opportunity to preserve
and recreate a lot of historic events in digital

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:32.680
form, maybe even use them in computer games,
and I am sure that artists will make great

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:34.470
use of such techniques.

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:39.120
Really well done, the paper is extremely well
written, the mathematics and the optimization

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:43.090
formulations are beautiful, it was such a
joy to read.

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:48.460
Regarding the future, I am pretty sure we're
soon going to see some pose and skeleton transfer

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:50.760
applications via machine learning.

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:55.770
The input would be a real-world video with
a person doing something, and we could essentially

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:59.560
edit the video and bend these characters to
our will.

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:04.110
There are some exploratory works in this area
already, the Disney guys for instance are

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:05.600
doing quite well.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:08.050
There will be lots of fun to be had indeed!

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:13.569
Also, make sure to check out the YouTube channel
of Welch Labs, who has a great introductory

00:03:13.569 --> 00:03:18.300
series for neural networks, which is in my
opinion, second to none.

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:22.760
He also has a new series called "Learning
to see", where he codes up a machine learning

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:25.190
technique for a computer vision application.

00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:28.500
It is about counting the number of fingers
on an image.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:29.840
Really cool, right?

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:34.400
The quality of these videos is through the
roof, the link for both of these series are

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.510
available in the description box, make sure
to check them out!

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:41.350
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

